@vsm 1.0
// docs/spec/system_certification/parameter_registry.vsm
// LIVING DOCUMENT — pillar 3 of certification_charter_2026-08-03.vsm.
// Status: SEEDED, INCOMPLETE BY DESIGN. Every system parameter that has (or
// should have) ONE canonical source gets a row here: what it is, where it
// lives today, whether it's centralized, and what breaks if it drifts.
// centralization_status ∈ {centralized, scattered, missing_entirely}.
//
// The generative pattern already proven to work (rotate_nats_token.sh:
// one canonical value, pushed to every registered consumer, never
// hand-edited per-location) is the TARGET shape for every row below marked
// "scattered". docs/INDEX.md (generated by docs_reconcile.py) is a second
// proof this pattern works when actually built -- cite it as precedent,
// don't reinvent the mechanism per parameter type.

registry("PARAMETER-REGISTRY-2026-08-03") {

  parameters: [
    { param: "NATS auth token",
      status: "centralized", canonical_source: "scripts/state/rotate_nats_token.sh + docs/spec/nats_token_registry.vsm registry V2",
      note: "The proof-of-concept for this whole pillar. Took 3 sweep passes to find all 19+ locations (2 missed on the first). Push mechanism exists and works." },
    { param: "measure(Var) — the variety measure, which determines the ⊕ operator in A6 (Ashby)",
      status: "centralized", canonical_source: "docs/spec/vsm_notation_spec.md Part I `⊕` row + A6 (declares the parametrization); VSF's instantiation = additive ⟹ ⊕ = +",
      note: "Was missing_entirely until 2026-08-04 and nobody had noticed: canon defined `⊕` only as 'Variety combination (Ashby)', never as an operation, so A6 was an axiom that could not be evaluated. The live value was recoverable ONLY from code — operational.rs computes dv_in = |Δcpu|+|Δmem|+|Δio| (additive over independent axes) and invariant_checker.rs VAR-1 tests ΔV_in−ΔV_out ≥ 0 (difference form), while TRUST-1 in the SAME file writes Π explicitly when it means multiplication. Meanwhile docs/theory/recursive_vsm_theory.md §6 asserted the OPPOSITE (Var(S3)·Var(S2)) for months with no code behind it. Ratified additive 2026-08-04 (L3/A5, rmw3). Textbook case for this pillar: an undeclared parameter is not merely undocumented — it silently splits into two contradictory 'canonical' answers, and the one with an implementation wins only if somebody goes and reads the implementation." },
    { param: "λ — trust-decay rate (Def_TrustUpdate EMA) and its INDEXING (shared vs per-level)",
      status: "centralized", canonical_source: "projects/vsf-nucleus/src/trust_axes.rs — LAMBDA_CALIBRATED=0.10, LAMBDA_MIN=0.05, LAMBDA_MAX=0.20, single shared entropy-adaptive λ. Empirical source: EXP-TRUST-01 run_4/phase B (2026-06-19), measured 0.10002 ± 1.8e-5",
      note: "The code was ALWAYS centralized and correct; the DOC was the drifted copy, and drifted for ~2 months undetected. vsm_notation_spec.md D11b carried per-level defaults (L0=0.02 L1=0.05 L2=0.15) that predate EXP-TRUST-01, match no code, and whose L0 value sits BELOW the deployed clamp floor (0.05) — unreachable by construction. Worse, on 2026-08-03 Claude found the doc's internal contradiction (ordering vs defaults), 'fixed' it by picking an inequality direction WITHOUT checking the implementation, and then escalated it into a ratified governance rule (Q-vsf-13) over a parameter structure that does not exist. rmw3 caught it one turn later ('quizas esos cambios ocultaron algunas operaciones que estaban correctamente resueltas'). Corrected 2026-08-04. Two lessons this pillar should institutionalize: (1) an internal contradiction in a doc is evidence the BLOCK is stale, not an invitation to pick a side; (2) 'resolved' must cite an implementation or an experiment, never only internal coherence." },
    { param: "LXC 101 baseline_mem (S3 resource bargain floor)",
      status: "scattered", canonical_source: "NONE -- hand-edited in scripts/s3_resource_alloc.py, drifted from live reality TWICE (1024->8192 fix 07-02, then live moved to 3072 on 08-01 and the file was never re-synced)",
      note: "Reopened as TASKS.vsm(A0-ALLOC-BASELINE-SYNC), task #18. The S3 bargain computes grants from this value -- a stale baseline makes every subsequent grant wrong, silently." },
    { param: "state.db LXC IP addresses",
      status: "scattered", canonical_source: "NONE -- state.db's lxcs.ip for 101 says 10.10.20.101, live reality (pct config + ARP) is 10.10.20.10",
      note: "TASKS.vsm(STATE-DB-101-IP-CANON-MISMATCH). Zero live-traffic impact TODAY only because nothing currently reads state.db's IP field for routing -- a latent trap for whatever eventually does." },
    { param: "cgroup resource limits (cpu.max / memory.max / io.max per LXC)",
      status: "missing_entirely", canonical_source: "NONE EXISTS -- not even a scattered one",
      note: "The actual boundary that let s1-clima consume 38x LXC 117's write I/O for 12+ hours undetected. thermal_resource_governance thread_4. This is the highest-value row in this table -- it's not drift, it's absence." },
    { param: "Emergency cgroup weights (per-LXC cpu.weight overrides)",
      status: "scattered", canonical_source: "NONE -- HANDOFF.vsm.md prose says 117=800/800, clima/110-ollama/cron=20/20 'still live'; live sysfs probe 2026-08-03 shows all three back at default 100",
      note: "TASKS.vsm task #22 (thread i). Nobody can currently say whether that revert was deliberate or accidental -- the value has no source of truth to check against." },
    { param: "io scheduler selection (mq-deadline vs bfq) per block device",
      status: "scattered", canonical_source: "NONE -- hot-switched live 2026-08-02, no persistence mechanism, no doc records which LXCs' io.weight semantics depend on it",
      note: "Silent footgun already confirmed once: io.weight is a complete no-op under mq-deadline. Whether bfq should persist is an open question in task #22." },
    { param: "VLAN / firewall / coupling config (per-LXC network topology)",
      status: "scattered", canonical_source: "NONE yet -- already scoped as target work, not built",
      note: "S1-ISOLATION-RELAY-ARCH decision_3 already names the target: 'state.db becomes the single GENERATIVE source for IP/VLAN/firewall/coupling config, derived artifacts (.fw, systemd-embedded iptables, interfaces, CLAUDE.md table) get generated not hand-maintained'. This registry adopts that design, doesn't duplicate it." },
    { param: "Axiom definitions (A0-A12, D-labels) and their formal statements",
      status: "scattered", canonical_source: "NONE -- currently spread across vsm_notation_spec.md, covenant.vsm, covenant_ops.vsm, vos_architecture.vsm, vos_kernel_spec.vsm, README.md, CLAUDE.md, each restating a subset",
      note: "docs/findings/axiom_canon_consistency_audit_2026-08-03.vsm open_question #5 ('single generated axiom registry, ID -> statement -> scope -> defining doc -> citing docs') is THIS EXACT ROW, found independently before this charter existed. Same pattern, same fix -- do not treat as a separate initiative." },
    { param: "docs/internal/PLAN.md Tier-α roadmap IDs (A0..A4)",
      status: "scattered", canonical_source: "PLAN.md itself, but bare 'A<n>' also appears un-namespaced in live Rust source (s5.rs, main.rs), colliding with the axiom row above",
      note: "TASKS.vsm CODE1 (task #23), DS currently auditing full scope. Not really a 'parameter' in the traditional sense, but the SAME centralization/namespacing failure mode -- listed here so it doesn't get lost between pillar 2 (cleanup) and pillar 3 (registry) framing." },
    { param: "LXC Proxmox container feature flags (nesting, keyctl, etc.)",
      status: "scattered", canonical_source: "NONE -- each LXC's `features:` line is set by hand at creation time, never audited fleet-wide",
      note: "Found 2026-08-03 during the fleet ghost-process sweep (task #28), triggered by rmw3 rejecting a too-quick 'disk contention' explanation for elevated PSI: LXC 104 (s5-policy) is the ONLY one of 8 running LXCs missing nesting=1. Breaks every systemd unit using mount-namespace sandboxing (ProtectSystem=strict/full) on that container -- confirmed: networking stack, logrotate (daily since 08-01), vsf-autoimmune. CRITICAL LATENT RISK: 4 of LXC 104's 5 core S5 services (algedonic/covenant/ledger/audit-sink) use the same directive and are currently 'active' only because they haven't restarted since before this broke -- TASKS.vsm(LXC104-MISSING-NESTING-S5-LATENT-RISK). This is the highest-severity row in this registry so far -- a hypervisor-layer config parameter, not an application one, but the exact same centralization failure shape." },
    { param: "docs/INDEX.md (file catalog)",
      status: "centralized", canonical_source: "scripts/docs/docs_reconcile.py --update, pre-commit-hook-enforced",
      note: "POSITIVE EXAMPLE, not a gap -- cite this as proof the generative-registry pattern is already working in this codebase for one data type (file inventory). Extend the SAME MECHANISM class to the other rows here rather than inventing N different ad-hoc scripts." },
  ],

  next: "This list is a STARTING seed. Every future finding that has the
      shape 'this value exists in more than one place and could drift' gets
      a row here, whether it's a secret, a network setting, a resource
      baseline, or an axiom definition -- the mechanism (one canonical
      source + generated push) is data-type-agnostic; don't let the
      registry itself fragment by treating each parameter class as a
      separate special case.",
}
